.ds PS P\s-2OST\s+2S\s-2CRIPT\s+2
.ds TS T\s-2RAN\s+2S\s-2CRIPT\s+2
.SH NAME
 psdman \- T\s-2RAN\s+2S\s-2CRIPT\s+2 document manager for P\s-2OST\s+2S\s-2CRIPT\s+2 printers
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.B  psdman
[
.B \-\s-1rfGFaL\s+1
][
.BI \-P printername
][
.BI \-p prog
][
.BI \-n user
][
.BI \-h host
][
.I file
]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.I psdman
is the \*(TS document manager, invoked by the printer interface script,
.I psinterface
(System V), or
.I psint.sh
(BSD),
after the printer interface program  has been invoked by the print spooler.
.PP
.I psdman
is responsible for  reading the the document file to be printed, and
taking the appropriate action to prepare the file for printing on a
\*(PS printer.
.I psdman
distinguishes between text files (which get formatted) and \*(PS print 
files.  If the input to 
.I psdman
does not begin with the \*(PS 
.B magic number
\- the first two characters being ``%!'' \-
.I psdman
will invoke
.I enscript
to create a listing of the file.
If the first bytes of the input file are ``%!PS-Adobe-'',
and if the printer options so specify,
.I psdman
will also will perform various document management tasks,
such as page reversal, font and other resource downloading, and
printer-specific feature inclusion (manual feed, duplex, paper tray
setting, etc.)
before printing, if so requested.
.I psdman 
supports LZW compression and ASCII85 encodingfor Level 2 printers.
.sp
The possible options are:
.TP
.BI \-r
never reverse.
.TP
.BI \-f
continue processing despite errors.
.TP
.BI \-G
do not attempt to rearrange fonts for more efficient downloading.
.TP
.BI \-F
turn off parsing.  If this is specified, psdman will simply pass the data
through untouched.  This means that it won't attempt to determine whether a
file is PostScript or not, either.
.TP
.BI \-a
strip out comments.  If this is specified, psdman will strip out any line
that begins with '%'.
.TP
.BI \-L
landscape.  Psdman will cause the input to be printed rotated 90 degrees.
It will not attempt to make sure the resulting image will fit on the page.
.TP
.BI \-P " printername "
.I printername
is the name of the \*(PS printer for which output is intended.
.TP
.BI \-p " program "
.I program
( BSD only) is the name of the program through which psdman was invoked.
Typically this is 
.IR psi0.
.TP
.BI \-h " host "
.I host
( BSD only) is the name of the host from which the print job originated.
.TP
.BI \-n " user "
.I user
( BSD only) is the name of the user for whom the print job is being printed.
.TP
.I  "file "
.I file
is the name of the document file to be processed by
.I psdman.
.SH ENVIRONMENT 
.br
There are a number of environment variables used by psdman
These are listed below.
The
.I psinterface
(System V) or
.I psint.sh
(BSD)
script sets the initial value for each,
and the printer options file can be used to change the value for a
particular printer.
Note that Bourne shell syntax must be used in the printer options file.
For BSD, this file is in the spool directory; for System V, look in
\fI/usr/spool/lp/transcript/ps.opt\fP.
Many of the variables take a number value, and specify whether
an action should take place or not; 1 means do the action, and
0 means don't do it.
.TP 
.BI REVERSE "=\fInumber\fP "
Reverse the page order
.TP
.BI VERBOSELOG "=\fInumber\fP "	
Print verbose log messages
.TP
.BI COMPRESS "=\fInumber\fP "
Perform LZW compression on file
.TP
.BI PSTEMPDIR "=\fIDirectory name\fP "
Directory for temp files
.PP
One could turn off page reversal for a particular printer
by adding this line to the \fIprinter\fP.opt file:
.br
\ \ \ \ REVERSE=0
.SH FILES
.TP
/usr/lib/transcript/bogusmsg.ps
\*(PS file for "spooled binary file rejected" message to printer
.TP
/tmp/t*
Temporary file to format text files.
